### MVC Architecture Pattern 
1. What is the MVC pattern and how does it help organize code? MVC (Model-View-Controller) is a design pattern that separates application logic into three components: - Model: Manages data and business logic. - View: Handles the user interface (not typically used in APIs). - Controller: Acts as an intermediary between Model and View, handling user input.
It organizes code by separating concerns, improving modularity, scalability, and testability. 
2. Responsibilities of each component in MVC - Controller: Processes incoming requests, validates input, calls services, and returns responses. - Service: Contains business logic (e.g., calculations, data transformations) and interacts with the Model. - Schema/Model: Defines the data structure (e.g., book properties like title, author) and handles data persistence (e.g., database operations). 
 3. How separating concerns in MVC improves maintainability and testability - Maintainability: Each component has a single responsibility, making it easier to update or refactor code without affecting other parts. - Testability: Isolated components (e.g., services or models) can be tested independently, enabling unit tests for business logic or data operations without involving HTTP requests.
